      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Cytokines such as IL-1beta, IL-6 and tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-alpha) have been shown to contribute directly to central and peripheral neuropathic pain. Recently, exogenous interleukin-10 (IL-10) was shown to impede development of dynorphin-induced allodynia presumably by inhibiting IL-1beta. We therefore wanted to determine whether endogenous IL-10 had a role in pain perception. By measuring the latency of the paw licking response, we show in IL-10 knockout mice and in normal mice treated with anti-IL-10 that latency times are increased, suggesting that endogenous IL-10 increases nociception. This does not appear to be directly correlated with IL-10's regulation of DREAM, a transcriptional regulator of prodynorphin synthesis."},
